  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "John Mathew vs Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Tuesday, The 12Th Day Of January 2016/22Nd Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1460 of 2005 ( ) --------------------------------- Cra 431/2003 of Addl.District & Sessions Court (Fast Track Adhoc)-Ii, Pathanamthitta Cc 69/2003 of C.J.M.Pathanamthitta Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused: ------------------------------------------------------------- John Mathew, Mallappallil, Omalloor P.O., Pathanamthitta. By Advs.Sri.Siby Mathew Sri.Philip J.Vettickattu Respondents/Respondents/Complainant: --------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Adoor Circle. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam. R1 & R2 By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 12-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - John Mathew vs Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/114492499/ 1 Crl.R.P. No.1460 of 2005 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 12th day of January 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 31.5.1999 at about 2.50 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased three packets of banana chips each weighing 200 gram. from the shop of the second accused. First accused was the licensee of the said shop. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P13 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for banana chips under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1956 and therefore, the same was adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P15 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would show that the sample showed the presence of water soluble synthetic colour tartarzine in violation of Rule 29 of Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1956 and therefore, it was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts. P1 to P26 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P14 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such John Mathew vs Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/114492499/ 2 laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P14 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such John Mathew vs Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/114492499/ 2 laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
